<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  WPML plugin configuration - read automatically by WPML when this plugin
  is active, to pre-fill Post Types Translation settings instead of
  requiring an admin to configure each one by hand.

  All four post types are non-translatable (translate="0") - a single
  canonical post per location/service/guide/booking, never duplicated per
  language. This took two attempts to get right, worth recording so it
  doesn't get re-litigated:

  1. Services and Guides were originally registered fully "Translatable"
     (translate="1"). That makes WPML create a SEPARATE post - a separate
     ID - per language, which is fine for ordinary content but wrong
     here: a Guide post isn't just content, it's tied to a stateful
     availability calendar (wp_tc_guide_availability, keyed by that
     post's ID) and to one specific WordPress login account
     (_tc_user_id). With three duplicate posts per guide, the calendar a
     guide manages through their own dashboard lived under ONE language's
     post ID, while a customer booking in a different language resolved
     a DIFFERENT post ID for "the same" guide - so a guide blocking a
     date only actually blocked it on one language version of the site.
  2. The fix attempt was "Display as Translated" (translate="2"), WPML's
     documented mode for "one canonical post, translator can still
     provide per-language title/content." Turned out this site's WPML
     Post Types Translation screen doesn't actually offer that as a
     choice for custom post types - only two flavors of full duplication
     ("Translatable - only show translated items" / "...or fall back to
     default language") or fully non-translatable. Confirmed by directly
     testing: switching to translate="2" in this file, then translating a
     Guide through wp-admin, still created a brand new duplicate post.

  Given "Display as Translated" isn't actually available, Service and
  Guide are now translate="0" like Location and Booking always were - a
  single, permanent, canonical post, guaranteed no duplication under any
  WPML setting on this site. Per-language TEXT (a guide's bio, a
  service's name/description, each extra's label/description) is instead
  registered with WPML's *String Translation* module at read time -
  TC_WPML::translate_string() in class-tc-wpml.php, called from
  TC_Rest_Api::get_services() / get_guide_for_location() /
  get_guides_by_location() - which translates a specific string
  independently of any post, with no duplication possible by
  construction. A translator fills these in under WPML -> String
  Translation, grouped under the "TC Booking Services" / "TC Booking
  Guides" / "TC Booking Extras" contexts.

  That single-post guarantee is also why TC_WPML::to_default_language_id()
  (used from save_guide() in class-tc-meta-boxes.php and get_guides_for()
  in class-tc-availability.php, for the Guide<->Location/Service ID
  assignments) is now a no-op for every post type this plugin registers.
  Left in place as a harmless defensive layer rather than ripped out.

  Verified end-to-end on the live/staging site for the "translation still
  creates a duplicate post" failure mode (that's what caught mistake #2
  above). NOT yet verified that WPML's String Translation module
  correctly picks up and serves the wpml_register_single_string /
  wpml_translate_single_string calls this plugin now makes - that's the
  next thing to confirm on a WPML-enabled staging site. See
  PROJECT_NOTES.md's "WPML support" section for the full history.
-->
<wpml-config>
	<custom-types>
		<custom-type translate="0">tc_location</custom-type>
		<custom-type translate="0">tc_service</custom-type>
		<custom-type translate="0">tc_guide</custom-type>
		<custom-type translate="0">tc_booking</custom-type>
	</custom-types>
</wpml-config>
